# Native data pack aggregation and operation ownership

Native captures now collect distinct encrypted file-data blobs in sequential data-only packs with a 16 MiB target. Tree blobs stay in separate packs. Pending and previously indexed identities deduplicate file data; a repeat capture preserves the existing data-pack IDs and index rows. The writer flushes pending data before publishing the referencing index and snapshot.

The application imports its own `restic-data-pack.mjs`. It never runs the original restic executable. The source-assisted packing contracts, exact upstream source and BSD notices are retained in the [study preservation manifest](results/pack-aggregation-followup/preservation-manifest.json), including the [upstream source provenance](results/pack-aggregation-followup/main-stage/source-provenance.json.snapshot). This slice does not reproduce original concurrent or randomized batching.

One public writer operation owns its queue. An overlapping `save`, `flush` or synchronous `dispose` rejects with `ERR_RESTIC_DATA_PACK_BUSY` before inspecting input or changing pending records. An internal automatic flush retains the enclosing save's ownership. A caller may retry a rejected operation after the owner finishes. A successful save snapshots caller bytes before awaiting a commit. Disposal clears queued ciphertext and permits subsequent use; it is not terminal shutdown.

Independent review found a real exported-helper defect in the initial candidate: a second save could report success while a first flush was held, then lose its accepted record when that flush cleared the queue. The reproduced failure and its exact `c68d66…` helper remain preserved. The guarded `6e83e2…` helper rejects that overlap. Four additional independent root comparisons reproduce the old loss and verify the guarded retry in both repository formats. No data loss in the prior application/HTTP path was demonstrated; that path awaited operations sequentially.

Commit failures preserve the primary error, clear owned pending ciphertext and release ownership. An entry guard failure can leave previously accepted pending records available for a safe retry. Guard expiry after a completed commit preserves that already committed object, rejects the incoming acceptance and clears pending references. Ciphertext cleanup does not promise to zero copied serialized packs, compressed/plaintext buffers, key material, native-library allocations or every runtime copy. A trusted commit callback is required.

The original prospective collection passed 66 checks; the production adapter passed 33; the final guarded helper collection passed 54, including 17 ownership cases. These are separate historical runs against separately fingerprinted sources. The current [integration receipt](results/pack-aggregation-followup/integration-validation.json) records the actual production collection and exact source identities. Its private HTTP check captures three files, confirms data-pack and index deduplication after restart, restores bytes and modification times, and runs two additional authenticated original check/restore commands.

The in-flight source-mutation fixture now uses a deterministic 24 MiB+1 input that crosses the aggregate target before source EOF. The previous 4 MiB fixture reached EOF before its observed pack publication after batching. Only the fixture changed; the original mutation/check/restore assertions remain. Two held small-file tests independently exercise mutation before publication and at the EOF sink boundary. These checks do not supply an atomic filesystem snapshot.

Input blobs are limited to 8 MiB and pending records to 4,096. A sole blob can exceed a smaller custom test target; ordinary batches remain within the configured target. Pending ciphertext, a serialized pack and an incoming encrypted blob can coexist during a held commit. Capture buffers, compression/encryption work, existing pack reads and runtime allocations add to that storage. The earlier 17 MiB calculation covers capture buffers only; it is not this writer's total memory or an RSS cap. No original speed or memory comparison has been performed for aggregation.

The existing two-minute cooperative native deadline cannot forcibly preempt blocked filesystem work or a commit promise. Failed captures can leave immutable unreferenced packs. Repair, native retention, remote backends, original command-line families, parallel scheduling and full metadata preservation remain open. Complete feature parity, security certification and universal superiority are unproven.
